WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.700
On this Hertzsprung Russell diagram, luminosity increases upward and

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:07.760
surface temperature decreases toward the right. Moving to the

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:11.700
right means cooler, not hotter. Use both coordinates to

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:14.500
interpret a star. At the same surface temperature, a

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:17.540
more luminous star has a larger radius. Watch two

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:20.340
stars share a temperature while their emitting areas and

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:23.620
positions differ. Giants can be cool and still very

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:26.960
luminous. At the same luminosity, a hotter star must

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:30.960
be smaller. Hot but faint white dwarfs occupy the

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:35.140
lower left. These comparisons show relationships, not the evolutionary

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:38.360
track of one star; the main sequence is not

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:40.272
a timeline from left to right.
